    "assumptions": [
      "The published 300 mm grip label has ambiguous endpoints: overall minus blade is 313 mm. The exposed leather is modeled as 250 mm, with a 51 mm pommel and 12 mm guard; the original museum field is retained rather than silently redefined.",
      "Blade full transverse height 16 mm, guard depth 24 mm, grip depths 16–20 mm and leather thickness 1 mm are unmeasured assumptions. Neither density nor hidden thickness is adjusted to match the published mass.",
      "The triangular blade is centered on its section centroid. Its precise section dimensions and orientation are not established by a calibrated axial photograph.",
      "Opposed terminal curls lie in a plane transverse to the blade axis. Their 16 mm nominal centerline radius and short straight cut ends are a bounded interpretation of differing photographic projections.",
      "Six contiguous wood/leather intervals describe one visible grip with three localized rib bands. Their matched faces partition the material; they do not assert six historical grip pieces or a known cord arrangement.",
      "The guard block and two quillons meet on complete finite surfaces as disjoint material regions. Blade and grip meet its opposing horizontal faces. Internal tang, peen, mortise, adhesive and fasteners are not reconstructed.",
      "The trumpet pommel is modeled as rotationally symmetric; its complete transverse section is not independently measured."
    ],
    "scope": "Complete visible structural sword: long three-sided blade and acute point, flared trumpet pommel, tapered grip with three separated rib bands, compact beveled block and thin opposed curled quillons. Corrosion, pitting, chips, torn leather and surface wear are excluded.",
